<section><div class="wide"><h2 id="configuration-inputs">Where values come from</h2>
<p>Your development mail catcher uses port 2525, but the production relay listens on 587. To tell which one your app will use,
  start with the sources you load into the factory. Then check what you set on the Mailer builder or the Jakarta Mail Session
  you supply. Email templates have their own timing and apply when you complete an Email or prepare it for sending.</p>
<p>With an explicit <code>ConfigLoader</code>, put the shared settings first and the deployment-specific settings last. The
  last non-blank value for each key wins. Blank or null entries leave the earlier value in place.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig config = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withMap("shared", Map.of("simplejavamail.smtp.port", 2525))
    .withMap("deployment", Map.of("simplejavamail.smtp.port", 587))
    .load();
Integer port = config.get(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT); // 587</code></pre>
<p><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults()</code> loads the conventional classpath file, then environment variables, then JVM system properties on its first call. <a href="/spring.html">Spring</a> follows its own Environment precedence, profiles and placeholder handling. These source choices resolve before your Java builder choices are considered.</p>
<p>A factory keeps what it loaded. Editing the original <code>Properties</code>, environment variables or JVM properties won't
  change its Mailers. Load the updated configuration and create a new factory and Mailers to use it. See
  <a href="/configuration.html#section-config-snapshot">replacing a configuration snapshot</a>. Restart a CLI daemon to pick
  up new startup settings.</p>
</div></section>
<section><div class="wide full-width-example">
      <h2 id="configuration-resolution-timing">When choices take effect</h2>
      <p>Say you build a draft while an order is being processed and send it later through one of several Mailers. The draft
        keeps the subject you supplied. The destination's template can still replace it when that Mailer prepares the send.
        Use the table to see when each choice takes effect. Setters can also check their arguments or do work immediately,
        such as reading signing material.</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>What it completes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td><code>ConfigLoader.load()</code></td><td>Reads the ordered sources and creates the factory's configuration snapshot.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>buildMailer()</code></td><td>Combines and checks choices for this Mailer and the named failover Mailers it creates, then constructs them.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>buildEmail()</code></td><td>Combines automatic-image choices, checks their effective settings and locks, resolves enabled image sources, and creates the submitted Email. Ordinary Email defaults and overrides remain unapplied.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>buildEmailCompletedWithDefaultsAndOverrides(...)</code></td><td>Explicitly applies the chosen Email governance during this call.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sending a composed Email</td><td>Applies the selected destination's Email defaults, overrides and locks during preparation for that send.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mailer.rehearse(...)</code> and <code>mailer.validate(...)</code></td><td>Use the invoked Mailer's own configuration. They do not select or validate a future cluster or failover destination.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Email import</td><td>The terminal operation combines captured import limits with its supplied <code>ImportLimitsConfig</code>, checks applicable locks and creates one allowance for that import and its selected nested content.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Conversion and export</td><td>Follow the governance chosen for that conversion. Fluent <code>fromEmail(...)</code> export does not implicitly apply factory Email templates; exporting a rehearsal uses its already-prepared snapshot.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Image URLs.</strong> Replace or clear an ordinary inherited image-base URL before
          <code>buildEmail()</code>. With URL resolution disabled, the builder leaves that base unparsed. With resolution
          enabled, a malformed active base fails the build before any image reads. Explicit String-to-URL conversion still
          checks its argument immediately, and mandatory locks still apply. See
          <a href="/email-composition.html#automatic-image-configuration-timing">image configuration timing</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Objects you already built.</strong> Builder edits affect future builds. A Mailer build can fail if its
          Session is in use or its settings conflict with an existing shared pool. On failure, the build cleans up resources
          it started for that attempt.</li>
        <li><strong>Import allowances.</strong> Reuse
          <a href="/email-conversion.html#conversion-import-limits">import limits</a> for multiple imports. Each import gets a
          fresh allowance. Getters inspect the supplied choices without consuming input or resolving defaults. Replacing the
          group discards its previous choices, omitted members inherit from the factory, and zero explicitly means unlimited.
          Fixed-value locks still apply. Later sends don't inherit the import limit.</li>
        <li><strong>S/MIME key reuse.</strong> Mailers and converters from the same factory share the
          <a href="security.html#section-smime-key-cache">S/MIME key reuse cache</a>. Separate factories from the same
          snapshot still have independent caches. By default (<code>-1</code>) the cache can retain any number of completed
          entries, <code>0</code> retains none, and a positive number sets the maximum. Creating an Email doesn't assign a
          cache or decide the future Mailer's policy. Replace the factory and the Mailers or converters using it to change
          capacity.</li>
      </ul>
      <pre><code class="language-java">Email submitted = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withSubject("Draft subject")
    .withPlainText("Draft body")
    .buildEmail();
// Subject and body still contain the submitted text.

mailer.sync().sendMail(submitted);
// The selected Mailer's applicable Email templates are applied during preparation.</code></pre>
      <p>A subject and a body use the same precedence. Recipients, headers and complete policies follow the
        <a href="#configuration-precedence">rules for each field</a>. The
        <a href="#connection-conflicts">connection conflict policy</a> applies separately from Email templates, pooling and
        execution. <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-exact-eml">Exact Email</a> preparation follows its own
        byte-preservation rules.</p>
    </div></section>
<section><div class="wide">
			<h2 id="configuration-precedence">When values replace or combine</h2>
			<table><thead><tr><th>Scope or value</th><th>Resolution</th></tr></thead><tbody>
				<tr><td>Property sources</td><td>Later non-blank values win. Blank or null entries do not erase an earlier value.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ordinary Mailer settings, such as pool size or legacy content support</td><td>Explicit Java choice, then captured configuration, then the published library fallback. Omitted group members inherit independently.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Supported connection settings</td><td>Different explicit declarations fail in strict mode. Deliberate override mode gives explicit typed Java members precedence over raw counterparts; omitted members retain applicable values.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Supplied Session gaps</td><td>Ordinary factory connection defaults leave omitted Session properties absent. Deliberate customization or applicable locks can add settings.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Email scalars and complete policy values</td><td>Applicable override, then submitted Email, then applicable default. Signing and encryption configurations are selected as a whole.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Recipients, Reply-To, delivery-recipient overrides, attachments and embedded images</td><td>Add missing override entries, retain every submitted entry, then add missing default entries. Submitted duplicates and their order remain intact.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Headers</td><td>Combine names case-insensitively. For one name, an override replaces its entire value list; otherwise submitted values replace the default list.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Recipient certificates and NOTIFY preferences</td><td>Group policy constructs the recipient metadata. The resulting explicit recipient value wins over the governance-resolved Email fallback, including an Email override.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Configuration locks</td><td>Separate mandatory restrictions remain binding through ordinary clears, template replacement and suppression.</td></tr>
			</tbody></table>
			<p><a href="/configuration.html#email-template-exclusions">Email template exclusions</a> determine which ordinary template contributions apply. Sending, rehearsing and exporting the original exact Email
				preserve its final bytes without ordinary templates or security transformations. Compatible envelope locks still apply;
				locks that would require changing the preserved content are rejected.
				See the <a href="security.html#section-sending-smime">recipient certificate rules</a> for the separate group-policy choices.</p>
		</div><h3>A subject, recipients and headers use different rules</h3>
<p>Suppose your app sends an order notice to a customer. The default template adds the audit mailbox, and the override adds
  the compliance mailbox and supplies routing headers. This example uses an empty factory configuration so you can see each
  contribution in the result.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(ConfigLoader.builder().load());
Email defaults = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withSubject("Default").to("audit@example.org")
    .withHeader("X-Route", "default").buildEmail();
Email overrides = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withSubject("Override").to("compliance@example.org")
    .withHeaders(Map.of("x-route", List.of("override-1", "override-2"))).buildEmail();
Email submitted = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withSubject("Submitted").to("alice@example.org").to("alice@example.org")
    .withHeader("X-ROUTE", "submitted").buildEmail();

try (Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder().withTransportModeLoggingOnly(true)
        .withEmailDefaults(defaults).withEmailOverrides(overrides).buildMailer()) {
    Email completed = mailer.getEmailGovernance().produceEmailApplyingDefaultsAndOverrides(submitted);
    // Subject: Override
    // To: compliance, alice, alice, audit (all at example.org)
    // X-Route, matched case-insensitively: [override-1, override-2]
}</code></pre>
<p>An empty submitted subject is still a supplied value and prevents the default from filling it. An eligible override can replace it.
  Clearing the local subject allows ordinary template fallback again. Complete signing/encryption configurations are selected together;
  their key, domain or other members are not assembled from different Email templates.</p><h3>Calendar choices and template exclusions</h3><p>A calendar's method and text belong together. Excluding either <code>CALENDAR_METHOD</code> or <code>CALENDAR_TEXT</code>
				skips that source's whole calendar. An override exclusion lets the Email's own calendar, or otherwise its default calendar, apply;
				a default exclusion leaves overrides eligible. Your Email's own calendar remains. If you excluded both selectors, allowing only one
				leaves the other exclusion active. Calendar transfer encoding is a separate choice.</p><div class="wide">
			<h3 id="repeated-email-completion">Completing an Email more than once</h3>
      <p>Say you build a receipt with no sender. During review, the review Mailer's template fills in
        <code>review@example.org</code>. When the receipt goes out, pass the original draft to the delivery Mailer so
        its own default can fill the sender.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">Email preview = reviewMailer.getEmailGovernance()
    .produceEmailApplyingDefaultsAndOverrides(receipt);
// Inspect the completed preview.

deliveryMailer.sync().sendMail(receipt); // Send the original draft.</code></pre>
      <p>If you send <code>preview</code> instead, its sender is now a supplied value. The delivery Mailer can replace
        it with an override. Its default can only fill a missing sender.</p>

      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Email template behavior on the second pass">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>On the second pass</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Sender, subject and body</th><td>Keep supplied values. Overrides can replace them and defaults can fill gaps.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Recipients, attachments and images</th><td>Keep the entries already present and add missing template entries.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Headers</th><td>Combine names case-insensitively. A winning override replaces all values for that name.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">S/MIME, OpenPGP and DKIM</th><td>Pick each policy as a whole. Protection is applied when MIME is prepared.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Exclusions and locks</th><td>Keep exclusions and enforce applicable locks on each pass.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Exact Email and frozen retry content have separate preservation rules. Keep the original exact Email
        when the submitted EML bytes must survive.</p>

      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>How recipients and resources match</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Recipients.</strong> With submitted <code>[A, A, B]</code>, overrides <code>[A, C, C]</code>
            and defaults <code>[B, D, D]</code>, the result is <code>[C, A, A, B, D]</code>. Reapplying the same templates
            keeps that order. A match includes the name, address, To/Cc/Bcc role, certificate and notification preferences.
            It accounts for a missing name filled by a lock. Explicit conflicting names still fail validation.</li>
          <li><strong>Attachments and images.</strong> A match requires the same metadata and a recognizable source, such as
            the same resource or DataSource, or the same library-owned path or bytes. Different filenames, descriptions,
            Content-IDs or encodings keep entries distinct. This check opens no files or remote streams. Opaque sources stay
            separate even with the same name and MIME type. Serialization or reconstruction can lose recognizable source identity.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Exact EML, confidentiality and evidence from received mail</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Exact EML.</strong> Calling
            <code>mailer.getEmailGovernance().produceEmailApplyingDefaultsAndOverrides(exactEmail)</code> applies ordinary
            templates to its parsed view and creates a composed Email. The original remains unchanged. The composed copy can
            change the original bytes and invalidate existing signatures.</li>
          <li><strong>Confidentiality.</strong> A draft keeps its confidentiality requirements and deliberate permissions.
            Templates cannot grant that permission or erase a requirement. A conversion's local permission stays with that
            conversion. Removing every automatically reused confidential component can remove the corresponding requirement.
            Editing a reply body can still leave the original quote in place.</li>
          <li><strong>Received-message evidence.</strong> Completion keeps captured sources, Problems, complete originals and
            evidence for unchanged fields and resources. A new template value has unknown incoming protection and doesn't
            inherit historical signature claims. Observations about how the original and its direct resources were opened
            still describe that input. A parsed child's body evidence stays with the child. Ordinary copies and supported
            snapshots preserve the same distinction. See the
            <a href="security.html#section-inspecting-received-protection">receiving workflow</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Forwarded message templates</summary>
        <p>A default can fill a missing forwarded message and an override can replace a supplied one. Use the per-field exclusions
          for <code>EmailProperty.MAIL_TO_FORWARD</code> to skip either contribution. The subject follows its own precedence.
          Keep the selected <code>MimeMessage</code> available and unchanged. Applying the template leaves its body unread and
          uncopied. Exact Email preparation bypasses those templates.</p>
        <p><code>clearEmailToForward()</code> removes a local source and keeps other fields and exclusions. A template can then
          supply the source again unless you excluded it. See
          <a href="/email-composition.html#clearing-forwarded-message">editing a forwarded draft</a>.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
</section>
<section>
  <div class="wide">
    <h2 id="configuration-operations">Changing an existing choice</h2>
    <p>The factory loaded <code>simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.maxsize=7</code>. Your worker needs room for 9
      connections and waits up to two seconds to claim one. It supplies both choices when configuring its pool.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">ConnectionPoolConfig pool = ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
    .withMaxSize(9)
    .withClaimTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
    .build();
mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(pool);</code></pre>

    <h3>Change the core size while keeping the maximum</h3>
    <p>Start from the current group to keep its maximum of 9 and its two-second claim timeout.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(mailerBuilder.getConnectionPoolConfig().toBuilder()
    .withCoreSize(1)
    .build());</code></pre>
    <p>If you pass a new group with only a core size, the maximum falls back to the factory's 7 and the claim timeout
      inherits too. A group setter replaces the group's supplied choices.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
    .withCoreSize(1)
    .build());</code></pre>

    <h3>Clear creation-age expiration or inherit it</h3>
    <p>Use <code>clearExpireAfterCreation()</code> to disable the creation-age limit, even if the factory set one.
      Use <code>useConfiguredExpireAfterCreation()</code> to let the factory's value apply again.
      Both edits keep the rest of the group, including any idle expiration.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">ConnectionPoolConfig noCreationAge = mailerBuilder.getConnectionPoolConfig().toBuilder()
    .clearExpireAfterCreation()
    .build();
mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(noCreationAge);

ConnectionPoolConfig inheritedCreationAge = noCreationAge.toBuilder()
    .useConfiguredExpireAfterCreation()
    .build();
mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(inheritedCreationAge);</code></pre>
    <p>Both group values return <code>null</code> from <code>getExpireAfterCreation()</code>. Check
      <code>isExpireAfterCreationCleared()</code> to tell the explicit clear from inheritance.</p>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Setters, resets and configuration getters</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Supply a choice.</strong> Use <code>withX(value)</code>. Group setters replace the prior group;
          collection methods can append where their contract says so. Previously built values and Mailers stay as they were.</li>
        <li><strong>Clear or restore.</strong> <code>clearX()</code> removes a choice at its documented scope. A fallback may
          apply. <code>useConfiguredX()</code> restores captured inheritance, as in <code>useConfiguredMaxSize()</code>
          for one member or <code>useConfiguredConnectionPoolConfig()</code> for the whole group. <code>resetX()</code> selects
          the built-in fallback, which may be absence. For example, <code>resetMaxSize()</code> selects the library's maximum.
          Available operations vary by setting. An empty Java string is an explicit choice subject to that setting's rules.
          Blank source entries are treated as absent.</li>
        <li><strong>Inspect a choice.</strong> Builder getters, including <code>getMailSendRetryPolicy()</code>, show the
          supplied groups without resolving settings or allocating resources. Group builders show the individual choices
          and clear flags. Use the built Mailer's <code>OperationalConfig</code> for resolved settings, including retry
          settings. It reports construction settings and leaves out later direct Session mutations.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    <details class="doc-disclosure" id="explicit-connection-clears">
      <summary>Explicit clears and connection conflicts</summary>
      <p>Clearing a hostname or local bind can remove the corresponding unlocked direct Jakarta Mail property,
        including a captured raw extra property. An imported value requires <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>
        for removal. Strict mode treats the removal as a conflict. <code>clearLocalBindAddress()</code> clears both
        the address and port. The port-specific clear keeps the address.</p>
      <p>Removing a Java choice on a supplied Session restores its baseline where applicable. Ordinary factory
        connection defaults still leave Session gaps alone. Unset choices inherit, and locks and restrictions on
        clearing inherited Session properties still apply. The connection conflict policy is separate from group replacement.</p>
    </details>

    <h3>Editing Email templates</h3>
    <p>Repeated <code>withEmailDefaults(...)</code> calls replace the template. Use <code>useConfiguredEmailDefaults()</code>
      to restore the snapshot-derived defaults or <code>withoutEmailDefaults()</code> to skip ordinary defaults. Locks still
      apply. For a single draft, use <a href="/configuration.html#email-template-exclusions">per-email exclusions</a>.</p>
    <p>To remove DKIM from the default template, materialize the configured defaults, copy that Email, call <code>clearDkim()</code>
      and give the result to <code>withEmailDefaults(...)</code>. Materialization still reads any configured signing key.
      The <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#dkim-defaults">migration example</a> shows both replacement and removal.</p>
    <p>To skip ordinary DKIM contributions for one draft, clear its local setting and exclude the default and override fields.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">EmailPopulatingBuilder draft = mail.emailBuilder()
    .copying(original)
    .clearDkim();

draft.withTemplateOptions(draft.getTemplateOptions().toBuilder()
    .dontApplyDefaultValueFor(EmailProperty.DKIM_SIGNING_CONFIG)
    .dontApplyOverrideValueFor(EmailProperty.DKIM_SIGNING_CONFIG)
    .build());</code></pre>
    <p>The original Email stays as it was. Editing its copied options keeps unrelated exclusions. Mandatory locks still apply.</p>
    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Add, remove or clear template exclusions</summary>
      <p>Repeated exclusion calls on <code>EmailTemplateOptions.Builder</code> accumulate, and an empty call changes nothing.
        Use <code>allowDefaultValueFor(...)</code> or <code>allowOverrideValueFor(...)</code> to remove selected exclusions.
        Use <code>clearDefaultValueSuppressions()</code> or <code>clearOverrideValueSuppressions()</code> to remove all exclusions
        in that family. The global <code>ignoringDefaults()</code> and <code>ignoringOverrides()</code> choices and configuration
        locks stay in effect.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
<a href="#connection-conflicts" id="connection-conflicts" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Conflicting connection settings</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>Suppose shared configuration sets one SOCKS proxy and your application chooses another. If both declarations reach
        <code>buildMailer()</code>, the default <code>ConnectionConflictPolicy.REJECT_CONFLICTS</code> stops construction
        before changing your Session. The error names the setting and the sources that supplied it.</p>
      <p>If you deliberately want Java to choose the proxy, select <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>.
        Here the worker replaces the shared proxy host and inherits its port.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withConnectionConflictPolicy(
        ConnectionConflictPolicy.ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES)
    .withProperty("mail.smtp.socks.host", "shared-proxy.example.org")
    .withProperty("mail.smtp.socks.port", 1081)
    .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
        .withHost("chosen-proxy.example.org")
        .build())
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
      <p>The effective endpoint is <code>chosen-proxy.example.org:1081</code>. Without the explicit override policy, the two hosts would
        conflict. In either mode, matching declarations can be combined, such as a host from Jakarta Mail and credentials from Simple Java Mail.
        <a href="#locked-requirements">Locks</a> and mandatory TLS requirements still apply.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>How declarations are compared</summary>
<p>Values from files, the environment and Spring resolve by source priority first.
        The policy compares explicit declarations for supported SMTP, proxy, timeout, local-binding, EHLO and TLS settings.
        Built-in defaults alone do not trigger conflicts. Custom provider properties and credentials held by an Authenticator follow their own rules.
        Explicit typed choices win over raw counterparts in override mode regardless of call order.</p>      <p>The policy accepts equivalent values. Trusted-host lists can differ in order or duplicates if the exact names agree.
        Socket factories and other object settings have to refer to the same instance. An empty string counts as a choice.
        Provider selection also matters. For the selected protocol, the policy checks <code>mail.smtp.class</code> or
        <code>mail.smtps.class</code>, including <code>Session.setProvider(...)</code>. Matching transport class names are
        accepted. To replace a different name deliberately, select <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>.</p>
      <p>A raw <code>mail.smtp.socks.host</code> can include a decimal port, such as <code>proxy.example.org:1081</code>. If
        that suffix is malformed and there is no separate port, the provider uses its default. A separate raw port wins and
        still accepts the provider's formats, including hexadecimal. A typed anonymous IPv6 proxy host retains its full
        address on an Angus connection managed by Simple Java Mail. Adding proxy credentials keeps the interpreted remote
        endpoint. An untouched supplied Session with no port continues to use the provider's fallback.</p>
      <p>Only String property names participate in Simple Java Mail's connection resolution. Non-String keys in a supplied <code>Properties</code> object remain opaque
        entries. For supported text properties, inspection and locks use the inherited String that Jakarta Mail sees, even behind a direct
        non-String value. Inherited socket-factory class names remain subject to the same TLS-lock restrictions as direct entries.</p>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Change or lock the conflict policy</summary>
<pre><code class="language-properties small"># Default; Java can select a different mode unless it is locked:
simplejavamail.connection.conflictpolicy=REJECT_CONFLICTS

# Alternatively, require strict handling within this factory:
simplejavamail.locked.connection.conflictpolicy=REJECT_CONFLICTS</code></pre>      <p>Repeated setters still replace earlier local choices, and replacing a group discards its previous supplied choices. The policy compares
        the completed choices, not setter history. Clearing an imported connection value is a conflict in strict mode; clearing a local-only
        choice remains normal builder editing. See <a href="#configuration-operations">clearing, resetting and restoring configuration</a>.</p>
      <p><code>getConnectionConflictPolicy()</code> on the builder inspects its explicit selection. <code>resetConnectionConflictPolicy()</code>
        selects <code>REJECT_CONFLICTS</code>; <code>useConfiguredConnectionConflictPolicy()</code> restores the captured policy, or the strict
        default if none was configured. Inspect the resolved mode with <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getConnectionConflictPolicy()</code>,
        or read the captured setting with <code>ConfigKeys.CONNECTION_CONFLICT_POLICY</code>. The CLI accepts
        <code>--mailer:withConnectionConflictPolicy ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>, and Spring accepts the same property values.</p>
</details>
    </div>
  </section><section><div class="wide"><h3>One configuration group can contain different rules</h3>
<p><code>ConnectionConfig</code> groups related API choices, but the conflict policy applies to supported connection declarations.
  Its legacy-content flag keeps ordinary Mailer precedence. Here, Java's legacy-content choice is valid in strict mode, while the different timeout conflicts.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig deployment = ConfigLoader.builder().withMap("deployment", Map.of(
    "simplejavamail.defaults.readtimeoutmillis", 10_000,
    "simplejavamail.smtp.legacycontentsupport", true)).load();
SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(deployment);
ConnectionConfig choices = ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .withReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(20))
    .withLegacySmtpContentSupport(false).build();
MailerRegularBuilder&lt;?&gt; builder = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 25).withConnectionConfig(choices);
// builder.buildMailer() would reject the different read timeout in strict mode.

builder.withConnectionConflictPolicy(ConnectionConflictPolicy.ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES);
try (Mailer mailer = builder.buildMailer()) {
    // Read timeout: 20 seconds. Legacy content support: false.
}</code></pre>
<p>If the Java value only supplied <code>withLegacySmtpContentSupport(false)</code>, construction would succeed in strict mode
  and retain the configured ten-second read timeout. Applicable locks constrain both examples.</p></div></section>
<section>
  <div class="wide">
    <h3 id="supplied-sessions">Using your own Session</h3>
    <p>Say your app server hands you a Jakarta Mail <code>Session</code> with <code>mail.transport.protocol=smtp</code>
      and <code>mail.smtp.timeout=30000</code>. Give the library that Session and you'll keep its 30-second read timeout.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">try (Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder(appServerSession).buildMailer()) {
    Duration readTimeout = mailer.getOperationalConfig().getReadTimeout(); // 30 seconds
}</code></pre>
    <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Read timeout outcomes for a supplied Session">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Builder choice</th><th scope="col">What happens to the 30-second setting</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Leave the timeout alone</th><td>The Mailer keeps 30 seconds. Ordinary factory defaults also leave gaps alone.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Choose 5 seconds in strict mode</th><td><code>buildMailer()</code> rejects the conflicting declaration before changing the Session.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Choose 5 seconds with <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code></th><td>The Mailer uses 5 seconds if no lock requires a different value.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
<pre><code class="language-java">try (Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder(appServerSession)
        .withConnectionConflictPolicy(ConnectionConflictPolicy.ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES)
        .withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder().withReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5)).build())
        .buildMailer()) {
    Duration readTimeout = mailer.getOperationalConfig().getReadTimeout(); // 5 seconds
}</code></pre>
    <p>The same <a href="#connection-conflicts">connection conflict policy</a> covers other supplied settings, including local binding,
      the EHLO name and certificate trust. Use one Mailer for concurrent sends. Wait for successful shutdown before giving the
      Session or its shared <code>Properties</code> to a different Mailer. Keep the Session stable while a Mailer owns it.</p>

    <details class="doc-disclosure" id="supplied-session-protocol">
      <summary>Inherited properties, protocol selection and custom TLS</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Inherited values.</strong> If a setting comes from a <code>Properties</code> default, the builder rejects
          a clear before changing the Session. Supply a replacement or create a Session with independent Properties.</li>
        <li><strong>Protocol selection.</strong> Set <code>mail.transport.protocol</code> to <code>smtp</code> or
          <code>smtps</code> on the Session or via the builder's <code>withProperty(...)</code> for protocol-specific options or
          inspection. Changing a supplied protocol follows the conflict policy and locks. With it unset, ordinary sends can
          still use the Session's selected transport, but protocol-specific settings stay unspecified and a connection probe
          returns unsupported without connecting. For custom protocols, use the provider's properties.</li>
        <li><strong>TLS.</strong> Configure custom SSL factories and opportunistic TLS directly on the supplied Session.
          Use a Session created by Simple Java Mail if the library should make those construction choices.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
<section>
  <div class="wide">
    <h3 id="locked-requirements">What locks protect</h3>
    <p>Suppose finance sends a finalized invoice to <code>ap@example.org</code> and needs an archive copy for each submission.
      Lock the BCC in the factory's configuration.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.locked.defaults.bcc.address=archive@example.org</code></pre>
    <p>The SMTP envelope now includes <code>ap@example.org</code> and <code>archive@example.org</code>, even if the
      message specifies an explicit delivery-recipient override. An exact EML can take that extra envelope recipient and
      keep its original bytes. A lock requiring a content change is rejected.</p>
    <p>Locks protect choices within this factory. Application code can create another factory or use another transport.
      Delivery, returning DSNs and downstream compliance depend on the rest of the route.</p>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Exact EML headers and signing requirements</summary>
      <p>A matching readable header can satisfy a lock as it stands. Duplicate Subject, From or Reply-To fields make a lock
        on that field ambiguous and are rejected. A single Reply-To field may contain multiple addresses. An opaque signature
        on the supplied bytes cannot establish that the required signing configuration was used.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Locked credentials, custom sending and DSN support</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Supplied Session.</strong> A caller-owned Session must satisfy the locks. When the factory locks the SMTP
          username or password, let <code>mailerBuilder()</code> create the Session. Even a matching visible username leaves
          room for an Authenticator or cached credentials to choose another account. A custom TLS configuration is rejected
          if the factory cannot check its TLS locks.</li>
        <li><strong>CustomMailer.</strong> The callback receives the governed message and owns its transport and delivery envelope.
          A lock on either, including archive recipients, is rejected. Content rules applied by Simple Java Mail still apply.</li>
        <li><strong>Clusters.</strong> Check both the invoking Mailer's restrictions and the selected destination's SMTP configuration.
          Ordinary sends, batches and open-connection sends all retain the message requirements.</li>
        <li><strong>DSN and TLS.</strong> Locked DSN requests require explicit provider support and a server that advertises DSN.
          A third-party adapter must reject unsupported requests in place of falling back to best effort. Connection TLS and onward
          REQUIRETLS are separate requirements.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
<section><div class="wide full-width-example"><h2 id="configuration-diagnostics">Trace an unexpected setting from source to Mailer</h2>
<p>The deployment file says to wait 30 seconds for an SMTP reply. Your invoice worker waits 45. Check the value the factory loaded,
  the choice the builder supplied, and the settings on the built Mailer.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig config = ConfigLoader.builder()
    .withMap("deployment", Map.of(
        ConfigKeys.SMTP_HOST.key(), "smtp.example.org",
        ConfigKeys.READ_TIMEOUT.key(), "30000"))
    .load();

var builder = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(config).mailerBuilder()
    .withConnectionConflictPolicy(ConnectionConflictPolicy.ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES)
    .withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
        .withReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(45))
        .build());

try (Mailer mailer = builder.buildMailer()) {
    System.out.printf("Loaded from %s: %s%n", config.getPropertySource(ConfigKeys.READ_TIMEOUT),
        config.get(ConfigKeys.READ_TIMEOUT));
    System.out.println("Builder: " + builder.getConnectionConfig().getReadTimeout());
    System.out.println("Mailer: " + mailer.getOperationalConfig().getReadTimeout());
}</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text">Loaded from deployment: PT30S
Builder: PT45S
Mailer: PT45S</code></pre>
<p>Here the Java code explicitly allowed the override. With the default conflict policy these two read timeouts would fail construction.
  If you expected the file to win, find and remove or align the conflicting declaration. Locks and transport requirements still apply.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lookup failed at load time?</strong> Check the property’s syntax and type. Use
    <a href="/debugging.html#section-config-diagnostics">redacted configuration diagnostics</a> for a log-friendly view of values and their sources.</li>
  <li><strong>Mailer built with a different value?</strong> Check Java builder choices and any supplied Session.
    <code>getOperationalConfig()</code> describes construction. For a message field such as Subject, check the selected Mailer’s templates at preparation time.</li>
</ul>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Interpreting omitted timeouts and server identity settings</summary>
<ul>  <li><strong>Socket timeouts.</strong> <code>ConnectionConfig</code> and its builder show the timeout choices you supplied.
    The built Mailer's <code>getOperationalConfig()</code> exposes the effective construction settings through
    <code>getConnectTimeout()</code>, <code>getReadTimeout()</code> and <code>getWriteTimeout()</code>. On either
    configuration type, <code>getSessionTimeout()</code> returns a duration when all three values are known and equal, and
    <code>null</code> otherwise. Check the individual values before interpreting <code>null</code>. The built Mailer getters
    can't see provider defaults or later changes made directly to the Session.</li>
  <li><strong>Server identity checks.</strong> On the built Mailer, <code>getVerifyingServerIdentity()</code> returns a
    <code>Boolean</code> for a known configured setting. You'll see <code>null</code> when a supplied Session omits the
    property or its value is unrecognized. The provider may still verify identity in that case. A Session created by Simple
    Java Mail reports the default it applies. <code>TlsConfig</code> and its builder show the stored choices. Check the live
    connection separately when you need to confirm its security.</li>
</ul><p>A builder getter inspects supplied choices. Provider defaults, private authentication state and later edits to a Session can affect the connection itself. Inspect a live connection to confirm negotiated security.</p>
</details>
</div></section>
